rror type, never the address or a header. The address is also refused when it holds a user name part (`@`) or a space, tab or line break, and unless the text between `//` and the first `/` is exactly a host name (letters, digits, dots, hyphens) or a bracketed IPv6 address, optionally followed by one `:port` with a port from 1 to 65535 (so a backslash, a second port, text after a bracket, an empty port and `:0` are refused, and a non-ASCII host name must be given in its ASCII form). n8n's own names such as `N8N_PORT` are n8n's, not this repository's; they appear in `docs/n8n-recipe.md`. `scripts/run_live_check.py` takes command-line flags, not settings names: `--key-file`, `--base-url`, `--ledger`, `--cap-run`, `--cap-total`, `--price-date` and one `--model ID=IN_PRICE,OUT_PRICE` per model, and optionally `--max-tokens`, `--effort`, `--timeout` (see the text at the top of the file).
 * `tools/scrub_n8n_export.py` (cleans an n8n export before it is published; no settings) replaces every address with a scheme (`://`, also with JSON-escaped slashes `:\/\/`), every `data:` link that has a media type, every `javascript:` or `vbscript:` link and every e-mail address that is not `example.test`, `example.com` or `example.invalid`. One kept exception: a web address (`http`, `https`, `ws`, `wss`) to `localhost` or `127.0.0.1` stays, up to the end of its path; its query string and fragment (`?...`, `#...`) are dropped, because an API key can sit in a query. An address with any other scheme to `localhost` (`file://localhost/...`, `postgres://localhost/...`) is replaced whole. A tab, a carriage return or a line feed right after a `localhost` host (a browser ignores it) turns the address into a look-alike: the address, the break and the next run of non-blank characters are replaced whole. Any other blank right after a `localhost` host (a space, a vertical tab, a form feed, a no-break space or another Unicode space; a browser keeps it in the user name) does the same, but only when the next run of non-blank characters holds an at-sign before any `/`, `?` or `#` (`http://localhost @host`); `http://localhost:5678/x y` and `http://localhost y` (words after a space) are not touched. Webhook ids (the id field) are never kept, but the webhook path is kept as written, so a secret inside a path stays. **Not cleaned** (read the output by eye before you publish it): an address without a scheme (`//host/path`, `host:5432`), a bare host name, a path that is not a URL, the scheme written with percent signs (`http%3A%2F%2F`) or with backslashes (`http:\\host`); a secret inside the path of a kept `localhost` address (`/webhook/<token>`), also as a `%3F`-encoded query inside the path (only a real `?` or `#` ends the path); a data: link without a media type (`data:,text`, `data:;base64,...`); an e-mail address written with the small at-sign U+FE6B or with an ideographic full stop in the domain; the keys of a dictionary inside node parameters (only the values are cleaned); and, in a node name, an expression that refers to another node by an old name when two names became the same (the second gets ` (2)`). Over-cleaning by design: an address that an expression completes, such as `"http://localhost:5678/x?" + $json.q`, is read as a plain address and loses its `?` and what follows on the same run of characters. Likewise a bare local address, a space and then an e-mail address (`http://localhost jane@example.test`) loses the e-mail address too.
 * `LEAD_DESK_LOAD_N` and `LEAD_DESK_LOAD_RETRY` are read only by one optional measurement test (`tests/test_server_hardening.py`, `test_measure_burst`): with `LEAD_DESK_LOAD_N=<number of simultaneous connections>` it fires that burst at a desk it starts itself and prints the status counts (`LEAD_DESK_LOAD_RETRY=1` lets the senders retry a refused connection); without `LEAD_DESK_LOAD_N` the test is skipped.
 * `DPLD_REQUIRE_SCAN` and `GITLEAKS_BIN` are read only by `tests/test_hygiene.py`. `DPLD_REQUIRE_SCAN` acts on any non-empty value (even `0`): it makes a missing gitleaks program a failure and also scans the git history. `GITLEAKS_BIN` is the path of the gitleaks program (if it is not set, the program is looked up on `PATH`). A hand-run secret scan must start in the repository root (`gitleaks dir .`, as `.github/workflows/lint.yml` does): the allowance for the lock files (`requirements-*.txt`) and the workflow files in `.gitleaks.toml` is anchored at the root, so a scan given an absolute folder path reports them.
 * `LEAD_DESK_PATH_ROOTS` (read by the load tools, `scripts/n8n_api.py import` and `tools/scrub_n8n_export.py`; not by the desk): extra folders, separated by `:` (`;` on Windows), where those tools may read and write the files named on their command line. Without it only the folder the tool was started in, the repository folder and the system temporary folder are allowed (`load_tools/README.md`). Not affected: `scripts/run_real_check.py`, which reads the config you give it from any folder (a file in a folder you can list), and the files named inside that config (`docs/real-services.md`, "Where the files may be").
 * `DPLD_REQUIRE_DEMO` is read only by `tests/conftest.py`. With `DPLD_REQUIRE_DEMO=1` a checkout that has no `demo_only` folder stops the test run at the start (exit code 2) instead of skipping the test files that need it (CI sets it, so a lost folder cannot make the run look green). Without it a missing folder skips each such test file as one item and the other files still run.
